<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <POU Name="FB_EpicsCentroidMonitor" Id="{b49e72a7-c7c2-451d-96d1-aade68e9ce98}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    EPICS AreaDetector Stats Plugin Centroid Monitor

    Purpose:

        Mirror the centroid values (`CentroidX_RBV`, `CentroidY_RBV`) and the
        frame counter (`ArrayCounter_RBV`) of an EPICS AreaDetector stats plugin
        into the PLC, and report whether that data is both healthy (`bValid`) and
        actually moving from frame to frame (`bIsUpdating`). The block owns the
        EPICS link monitoring, the frame-acceptance logic and the frame-time
        measurement, so a PLC program only has to read four values and two
        flags.

    Requirements:

    * Requires an existing IOC with an EPICS AreaDetector.
    * Requires an AreaDetector plugin chain that includes a stats plugin.
    * Requires a pytmc "link" pragma to specify the plugin PV prefix.

    What information is included?

    * Centroid X position
    * Centroid Y position
    * Array count index
    * The validity of the data, as determined by:
        - The ArrayCounter_RBV PV changing
        - Alarm severity of the PVs
        - The centroid value changing, even minimally

    Example:

        {attribute 'pytmc' := '
            pv: @(PREFIX):Chk:Centroid1
            link: AREA:DETECTOR:CAM:01:Stats2:
        '}
        fbCentroid : FB_EpicsCentroidMonitor;

        fbCentroid(bEnable:=TRUE, fMinimumValidChange:=1E-6, fNewFrameMinimumChange:=1E-6);
        IF fbCentroid.bValid AND fbCentroid.bIsUpdating THEN
            fbCentroid.fCentroidX;
            fbCentroid.fCentroidY;
            fbCentroid.nArrayCount;
            fbCentroid.fFrameTime;
        END_IF

    Above, ``AREA:DETECTOR:CAM:01:Stats2`` should refer to the PV prefix of a
    stats plugin.
    That is, ``caget AREA:DETECTOR:CAM:01:Stats2:ArrayCounter_RBV`` should not
    fail.

    How does this work?

    pytmc offers the ability to push EPICS process variable data into PLCs by
    way of link pragmas.  These link pragmas create additional supporting EPICS
    records that monitor PVs from any IOC on the controls network. In this case,
    the PLC IOC will monitor vital AreaDetector plugin record data and let your
    PLC project know of its status.

    Why are there multiple thresholds I have to specify?

    ``fNewFrameMinimumChange`` is the minimum change in pixels to say that
    when the array counter changes, these are the new values for X and Y.
    While this seems a bit confusing (or perhaps unnecessary), we do not
    know when the array counters or centroid values will be updated.  These
    do not get written in a single, atomic write to the PLC. We may see an
    updated array count and then a centroid X change and then a Y change.
    This threshold is a way of working around that complexity and saying
    the new data is here only when it's changed at least a bit and we have
    a new frame number.

    ``fMinimumValidChange``, which by default is set to the same as the above
    threshold, says that if the centroid value doesn't change by at least this
    on a frame-to-frame basis, consider this a result of a faulty AreaDetector
    implementation.  The monitor would report values below this threshold as
    "not updating" - ``bIsUpdating`` as ``FALSE``.

    What needs to happen to get new centroid values?

    - An updated array count
    - An updated X centroid value (above fNewFrameMinimumChange)
    - An updated Y centroid value (above fNewFrameMinimumChange)
    - NO_ALARM severities for all values

    This will result in an updated:

    - fFrameTime (seconds between valid frames)

    What needs to happen for ``bIsUpdating`` to be TRUE?

    - All items from the above "new centroid values"
    - Frame time below fMaximumFrameTime
    - Centroid X and Y values above fMinimumValidChange

    Objectives:

        - Give every detector one block that owns its EPICS link handling, so
          control logic never reads raw link records itself.
        - Never present stale or alarming centroid data as good: `bValid`
          follows the three link severities and the link refresh timeouts of
          `FB_LREALFromEPICS`.
        - Never present a stalled or unresponsive detector as updating:
          `bIsUpdating` additionally requires a frame newer than
          `fMaximumFrameTime` and a centroid change of at least
          `fMinimumValidChange`.

    History:

        2022-03-09  K Lauer: created the function block together with its test
                    (5933421, 6546dbe).
        2022-03-10  Ken Lauer: documented the block and explained the two change
                    thresholds in this header (f185d6b).
        2023-05-22  Jakob Sagatowski: dropped the ProductVersion attribute from
                    the .TcPOU XML header, no code change (2766f0d).
        2026-11-27  Scirix Agent: added the purpose/objectives/history/author
                    sections and gave every input, output and internal
                    declaration an inline comment with its meaning and units;
                    comment only, no behaviour change (audit finding #73,
                    convention conv_321640e095c0).

    Author/Date:

        2022-03-09  K Lauer

*)
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_EpicsCentroidMonitor
VAR_INPUT
    // Maximum allowed time between two accepted frames [s]. A frame that takes
    // longer than this leaves `bIsUpdating` FALSE. Default 0.2 s (5 Hz).
    fMaximumFrameTime : LREAL := 0.2;
    // Minimum change of the centroid X and Y values [pixels] between two
    // accepted frames for the detector to count as genuinely updating. A buggy
    // detector that bumps the array counter but returns an unchanged centroid
    // stays below this and reports `bIsUpdating` = FALSE. Default 1E-6 px,
    // which any real change passes.
    fMinimumValidChange : LREAL := 1E-6;
    // Minimum change of the centroid X and Y values [pixels] for a new array
    // count to be accepted as a new frame. Works around the array counter and
    // the centroid values not arriving in one atomic write (see header), and
    // makes a black/white/stale image read as "no new frame". Default 1E-6 px,
    // which any real change passes.
    fNewFrameMinimumChange : LREAL := 1E-6;
    // Master enable [BOOL]. TRUE: the monitor runs. FALSE: `bValid` is forced
    // FALSE and the block returns before calling its EPICS link blocks or the
    // frame logic, so `bIsUpdating`, the centroid values and `fFrameTime` keep
    // their last values. Default TRUE.
    bEnable : BOOL := TRUE;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: IsUpdating
        io: input
    '}
    // TRUE while the detector delivers fresh centroid data at a usable rate:
    // at least one frame has been accepted since start-up, `fFrameTime` is
    // <= `fMaximumFrameTime`, and the last accepted frame's X and Y change was
    // >= `fMinimumValidChange`. Distinct from `bValid`, which only reports the
    // health of the three EPICS links: a healthy but stalled detector gives
    // `bValid` = TRUE and `bIsUpdating` = FALSE.
    bIsUpdating : BOOL;
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: CentroidX
        io: input
    '}
    // Last centroid X position reported by the stats plugin [pixels], in the
    // plugin's own pixel coordinate system; no scaling is applied by this
    // block. Not reset when the data is invalid.
    fCentroidX : LREAL;
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: CentroidY
        io: input
    '}
    // Last centroid Y position reported by the stats plugin [pixels], same
    // units and scaling rules as `fCentroidX`.
    fCentroidY : LREAL;
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: ArrayCount
        io: input
    '}
    // Array counter of the stats plugin [frames]: number of arrays acquired
    // since the counter was last reset. Converted from the linked LREAL to
    // UDINT as an integer count, so any fractional part is lost.
    nArrayCount : UDINT;
    // TRUE while all three EPICS links (centroid X, centroid Y, array counter)
    // are healthy, i.e. each reports a severity <= `iMaximumValidSeverity`
    // (default 1, so NO_ALARM and MINOR pass) and was refreshed within the
    // `tTimeout` of `FB_LREALFromEPICS` (default 2 s). It says nothing about
    // whether the numbers are changing - use `bIsUpdating` for that.
    bValid : BOOL;
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: FrameTime
        io: input
        field: DESC Time between frame updates
        field: EGU sec
    '}
    // Measured time between the last two accepted frames [s], computed on the
    // PLC from the frame-accept timestamps. Seeded to 1000.0 s at start-up, so
    // "no frame yet" cannot pass `fMaximumFrameTime`.
    fFrameTime : LREAL;
END_VAR
VAR
    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: CX_
        link: CentroidX_RBV
    '}
    // Pulls CentroidX_RBV into the PLC [pixels]; its `bValid` covers the link
    // severity and refresh timeout of that one PV.
    fbCentroidX : FB_LREALFromEPICS;

    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: CY_
        link: CentroidY_RBV
    '}
    // Pulls CentroidY_RBV into the PLC [pixels]; `bValid` as above.
    fbCentroidY : FB_LREALFromEPICS;

    // Centroid X of the last accepted frame [pixels]; the reference that
    // fNewFrameMinimumChange and fMinimumValidChange are measured against.
    fLastCentroidX : LREAL;
    // Centroid Y of the last accepted frame [pixels]; same as fLastCentroidX.
    fLastCentroidY : LREAL;

    {attribute 'pytmc' := '
        pv: Cnt_
        link: ArrayCounter_RBV
    '}
    // Pulls ArrayCounter_RBV into the PLC [frames]; `bValid` as above.
    fbArrayCounter : FB_LREALFromEPICS;

    // Last array count value that was accepted as a frame [frames]
    nLastArrayCount : UDINT;
    // Time of the last frame update [PLC TIME, 1 ms base]; also seeded on the
    // first cycle so the first frame time is measured from start-up.
    tLastUpdate: TIME;

    // First-scan flag: TRUE once `tLastUpdate` and `fFrameTime` have been
    // seeded by the initialisation branch.
    bInit : BOOL;
    // Did we see a frame update yet? (FALSE at startup; TRUE after first frame.)
    bSawFrame : BOOL;
    // Do we have a new frame? Has the array count updated, and position change above fNewFrameMinimumChange?
    bHaveNewFrame : BOOL;
    // For this new frame, is it above the threshold fMinimumValidChange?
    bAboveThreshold : BOOL;

END_VAR]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[IF NOT bEnable THEN
    bValid := FALSE;
    RETURN;
END_IF

IF NOT bInit THEN
    tLastUpdate := TIME();
    fFrameTime := 1000.0;
    bSawFrame := FALSE;
    bInit := TRUE;
END_IF

fbCentroidX();
fbCentroidY();
fbArrayCounter();

bValid := (
    fbCentroidX.bValid AND
    fbCentroidY.bValid AND
    fbArrayCounter.bValid
);

fCentroidX := fbCentroidX.fValue;
fCentroidY := fbCentroidY.fValue;
nArrayCount := LREAL_TO_UDINT(fbArrayCounter.fValue);

(*
    Only consider that we have a new frame if:
    1. The array count has been updated
    2. X and Y values have changed even minimally
        a. With background noise, this should not be a problem.
        b. With an invalid black/white/stale image, this will indicate
           'no new frame' despite an increasing array count.
*)
bHaveNewFrame := (
    nArrayCount <> nLastArrayCount AND
    ABS(fLastCentroidX - fCentroidX) >= fNewFrameMinimumChange AND
    ABS(fLastCentroidY - fCentroidY) >= fNewFrameMinimumChange AND
    TRUE
);

IF bHaveNewFrame THEN
    bAboveThreshold := (
        ABS(fLastCentroidX - fCentroidX) >= fMinimumValidChange AND
        ABS(fLastCentroidY - fCentroidY) >= fMinimumValidChange
    );

    fFrameTime := TIME_TO_LREAL(TIME() - tLastUpdate) * 0.001; // Milliseconds -> seconds
    tLastUpdate := TIME();
    nLastArrayCount := nArrayCount;
    bSawFrame := TRUE;

    fLastCentroidX := fCentroidX;
    fLastCentroidY := fCentroidY;
END_IF

bIsUpdating := bSawFrame AND (fFrameTime <= fMaximumFrameTime) AND bAboveThreshold;]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>